    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. one more thing though—i've read some weird stuff online about how sometimes a starter just \"goes bad\" or gets really weird smells/molds even when you follow the rules. biologically, is there a way for the balance to just completely flip so that the \"good\" microbes get overtaken by something else, or is it more about how the environment changes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, the balance within a sourdough starter can indeed shift, and several factors might contribute to this. Biologically, an imbalance may arise if a detrimental strain, such as a mold or an out-of-balance mixture of yeasts and bacteria, gains an advantage over the beneficial ones like Saccharomyces cerevisiae and lactobacillus. This can happen through environmental changes or mishaps in your starter maintenance routine, such as overfeeding, neglecting to feed at regular intervals, leaving the starter in the wrong conditions for too long, or even due to an accidental contamination with foreign microbes from the surrounding environment, such as mold spores present in your workspace.\n\n\nIn an ecological sense, just as in a diverse ecosystem where one species can dominate due to environmental pressures, the sourdough starter microenvironment can shift in balance. This shift can be influenced by temperature fluctuations, feeding inconsistencies, and changes in the flour's nutrient composition. However, remember that robust starters do have mechanisms to self-regulate and often to resist these disturbances to an extent. Proper troubleshooting, such as adjusting feedings or altering the temperature, can help in reestablishing the proper balance and maintaining a healthy starter that continues to contribute to flavorful, nutritious sour",
      "source": "target",
